'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hanc', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f4e99fe-64d9-54c5-9b4b-5045dee3e76c', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f4e99fe-64d9-54c5-9b4b-5045dee3e76c'}, {'abstract': 'Selected by Rita Dove.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/01/magazine/poem-self-portrait.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Rita Dove.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Self-portraits in poetry are legion. Not only do they offer convenient writing exercises, but the process of describing yourself to strangers they can provide a lesson in identity politics. The speaker in Eloisa Amezcua’s scathing analysis, however, turns the tables (and the screws) by becoming the mirror, looking out — and what she sees is far from pretty. Whether looking for an affirmation that’s constantly shifting or holing up in furious retreat behind the glass, no one emerges victorious from the combat between the observer and the observed: Reflection becomes its own condemnation. Selected by Rita Dove.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 481, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 481}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/04/magazine/04mag-poem-image1/04mag-poem-image1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: Self-Portrait', 'kicker': 'Poem', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Self-Portrait', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-01T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eloisa Amezcua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eloisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amezcua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2afd7fe5-4ad2-5709-8f92-fe1f472a6add', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2afd7fe5-4ad2-5709-8f92-fe1f472a6add'}, {'abstract': 'The Scottish artist Katie Paterson is collecting 100 unpublished works that won’t be released in their writers’ lifetimes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/01/t-magazine/future-library-books.html', 'snippet': 'The Scottish artist Katie Paterson is collecting 100 unpublished works that won’t be released in their writers’ lifetimes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a small clearing in the forests of Nordmarka, one hour outside the city limits of Oslo, a thousand spruce trees are growing. They will grow for the next 96 years, until 2114, when they will be felled, pulped, pressed and dyed to serve as the paper supply for the Scottish artist Katie Paterson’s Future Library: an anthology of 100 previously unpublished books written by some of the 21st century’s most celebrated writers. There will be one book for every year the trees will have grown, each a donation from a writer chosen by the Future Library’s board of trustees — a gift from the literary gatekeepers of the present to the readers of the future.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/29/t-magazine/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/29/t-magazine/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/29/t-magazine/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 830, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/29/t-magazine/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1661, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/29/t-magazine/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5/29tmag-future-library-slide-PDP5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'heig