,” a family drama that opens with a mysterious image of death: The camera looks up silently at two women gently saying goodbye to an unseen patient. We do not know yet who the patient is; the answer to that question will turn out to be heartbreaking.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1228, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/30/arts/grief1/grief1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘The Grief of Others,’ Mourning Becomes Eclectic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Grief Of Others', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wang, Patrick (Film Director)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Leah Hager', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Grief of Others (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-01T11:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bilge Ebiri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bilge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ebiri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf3de08e-7047-521e-a20e-79839c530c84', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf3de08e-7047-521e-a20e-79839c530c84'}, {'abstract': 'A new documentary featuring interviews with collaborators and critics makes clear that Bergman, for all his depth, was also an entertainer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/01/movies/searching-for-ingmar-bergman-review.html', 'snippet': 'A new documentary featuring interviews with collaborators and critics makes clear that Bergman, for all his depth, was also an entertainer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year marks the centennial of Ingmar Bergman’s birth. The Swedish playwright, theater director and filmmaker, who died in 2007, remains one of the most praised and, to a certain extent, most misunderstood 20th-century artists. The praise stems from his cinematic mastery and treatment of profound themes; the misunderstanding, from the conventional wisdom that because Bergman treated profound themes, his work must be a slog.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/02/arts/02searching2/02searching2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xla