ment_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18f83ac3-35a7-5f74-be46-2ef1fa2c5b83', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18f83ac3-35a7-5f74-be46-2ef1fa2c5b83'}, {'abstract': 'Tara Fares fashioned herself into the Instagram queen of Iraq. Then she was shot to death. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/01/opinion/iraq-instagram-queen-tara-fares-murder-celebrity.html', 'snippet': 'Tara Fares fashioned herself into the Instagram queen of Iraq. Then she was shot to death. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct. 9, the Iraqi author Sinan Antoon tweeted, “Killing those who are other/different has become a ritual/hobby, practiced by individuals for fun, after having been institutionalized by the state, political parties and militias for years.” His tweet referred to reports that an Iraqi teenager had been butchered on a Baghdad street by an assailant who thought he looked gay. But Mr. Antoon could have been referring to any of the young Iraqis who have tried to break free of the rigid social codes governing both genders only to suffer vicious reprisals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/31/opinion/31crabapple/merlin_144750111_a1aefd68-740a-435c-8e08-2bae37e316af-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/31/opinion/31crabapple/merlin_144750111_a1aefd68-740a-435c-8e08-2bae37e316af-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur