'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry J. Farrell and Abraham L. Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Abraham', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/46aefa25-2384-5f9b-aef4-4f4c30f147e5', 'word_count': 919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46aefa25-2384-5f9b-aef4-4f4c30f147e5'}, {'abstract': 'She and political strategists like Jessica Byrd and Kayla Reed are designing a new theory of the Democratic coalition. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/01/opinion/stacey-abrams-governor-georgia.html', 'snippet': 'She and political strategists like Jessica Byrd and Kayla Reed are designing a new theory of the Democratic coalition. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'For too long, the Democratic Party has been comfortable with black women only running conventions or registering voters — doing background work. The party expects black women to be its backbone, as when 98 percent of black female voters in Alabama cast their ballots for Doug Jones in the special Senate election last year. But it has never truly supported them as leaders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/31/opinion/31Cooper-sub/31Cooper-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/31/opinion/31Cooper-sub/31Cooper-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/31/opinion/31Cooper-sub/31Cooper-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subTyp