erson': [{'firstname': 'Sopan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Deb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2c10a3b-6b36-5830-9c05-ea43c79b1d4b', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2c10a3b-6b36-5830-9c05-ea43c79b1d4b'}, {'abstract': 'If you’ve committed a crime and are rich, you can pay court fees and cast a ballot. If you don’t have money, you might be left out of democracy. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/01/opinion/election-voting-rights-poverty.html', 'snippet': 'If you’ve committed a crime and are rich, you can pay court fees and cast a ballot. If you don’t have money, you might be left out of democracy. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Election Day approaches, triggering feverish drives to turn out new voters, over six million people will be denied the right to register and cast a ballot this November. That’s almost 3 percent of the United States’ adult population.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/01/opinion/01langsebastian/01langsebastian-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/01/opinion/01langsebastian/01langsebastian-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/01/opinion/01langsebastian/01langsebastian-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/01/opinion/01langsebastian/01langsebastian-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1707, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 