nd some of the best vintage shopping in North America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1535, when Jacques Cartier, the first European to set eyes on what is now Canada, pushed inland from the shore of Quebec City to Montreal (then called Hochelaga), the French explorer’s companions issued a grave warning: “There would be so much ice and snow that all would perish.” Centuries later, Montreal still has one of the coldest climates north of the 49th parallel, and the visitors who descend on the city all but disappear in the wintertime. Yet for its residents Montreal’s charisma never dims, and with its affordable rents and thriving food scene — where a $10 gravy-soaked poutine from La Banquise and the foie gras at the gourmand-beloved institution Joe Beef command equal respect — it’s not hard to see why the city is home to so many writers, artists and musicians.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/01/t-magazine/travel/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/01/t-magazine/travel/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/01/t-magazine/travel/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/01/t-magazine/travel/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI/1tmag-montreal-slide-XAUI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': No