n Suskind', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suskind', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb80bb7a-0f0b-5d19-ae60-2cc6d2ea7c8d', 'word_count': 1322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb80bb7a-0f0b-5d19-ae60-2cc6d2ea7c8d'}, {'abstract': 'Germany is again divided between east and west. To understand why, it helps to look at eastern men, who lost their jobs and their country as many women fled west.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/05/world/europe/merkel-east-germany-nationalists-populism.html', 'snippet': 'Germany is again divided between east and west. To understand why, it helps to look at eastern men, who lost their jobs and their country as many women fled west.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EBERSBACH-NEUGERSDORF, Germany — Frank Dehmel was on the streets of East Germany in 1989. Every Monday, he marched against the Communist regime, demanding freedom and democracy and chanting with the crowds: “We are the people!”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Legacy of Merkel? Angry East German Men Fueling the Far Right', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': ' Angry East German Men Fuel Far Right in Nation Still at Odds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alternative for Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pegida (Germany)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_da