document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdee1d0d-3483-57e2-b23b-bbe15556457b', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdee1d0d-3483-57e2-b23b-bbe15556457b'}, {'abstract': 'At last week’s DealBook conference, companies and executives agreed that corporations can no longer operate without considering their larger societal impact.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/business-reaches-a-turning-point.html', 'snippet': 'At last week’s DealBook conference, companies and executives agreed that corporations can no longer operate without considering their larger societal impact.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I think as a group of business leaders, we have to think about the impact to our society of what’s happening in the political discussions in our country.” That’s what Kenneth C. Frazier, the chief executive of Merck & Co., said last week, just days before the midterm elections. It was the crystallization of a theme that has emerged louder than ever over the past several years among a c-suite that for much of the last half century tried to avoid politics and considered its role in society in simple terms: delivering profits to shareholders, often at the expense of other constituencies.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 514, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 514}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 992, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 992, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/06Dealbook-online-art/merlin_146362095_b4750917-a4be-445f-b55c-9af582e9b537-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Business Reaches a Turning Point', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At a Turning Point', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Hazards and Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorkin, Andrew Ross', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-06T07:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ross Sorkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00090159-300e-5852-afe7-f39e090b700d', 'word_count': 1061, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00090159-300e-5852-afe7-f39e090b700d'}, {'abstract': 'Contractors like Uber drivers can’t receive stock in private companies. But that could soon change, in a move supported by tech companies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/06/business/dealbook/gig-economy-equity-sec-rule-701-uber-airbnb.html', 'snippet': 'Contractors like Uber drivers can’t receive stock in private companies. But that could soon change, in a move supported by tech companies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The gig economy has created economic opportunities for millions of people. Uber and Lyft have empowered everyday drivers to become paid chauffeurs. Airbnb has made homeowners hoteliers. And TaskRabbit has opened new markets for handymen and other laborers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/business/06gelles-1/merlin_142182702_a884c691-a1fa-4172-822c-0ea861cf818d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/06/business/06gelles-1/merlin_142182702_a884c691-a1fa-4172-822c-0ea861cf818d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url