'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jugal', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Patel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a92ba284-a86c-585a-9069-2bece97aeeb3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a92ba284-a86c-585a-9069-2bece97aeeb3'}, {'abstract': 'It’s easy to nostalgize some postwar golden age of political consensus. But the divisions that followed were more intentional than some might think.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/06/magazine/do-americans-dislike-partisan-politics-or-just-other-partisans.html', 'snippet': 'It’s easy to nostalgize some postwar golden age of political consensus. But the divisions that followed were more intentional than some might think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to think of a figure whose place in public life has been scrambled quite so fully in the past two years as John Brennan’s. As recently as last year, Brennan was serving as President Obama’s C.I.A. director — a political appointment that, by virtue of its awesome power, is supposed to exist at some remove from partisan politics. This has subsequently made Brennan one of the more alarmed and strident critics of Donald Trump — a president for whom the idea of anything existing outside partisan politics seems inconceivable — and of his Republican allies in Congress.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-FirstWords-1/11mag-FirstWords-1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Americans Dislike ‘Partisan’ Politics, or Just Other Partisans?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Squad Goals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liberalism (US Politics)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rusher, William A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-06T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Homans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Homans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c7fab33-e035-588c-957d-172513411f49', 'word_count': 1452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c7fab33-e035-588c-957d-172513411f49'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on leaving your addicted son money and reporting a teacher who is duplicating course material.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/06/magazine/can-we-disinherit-our-addicted-son.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on leaving your addicted son money and reporting a teacher who is duplicating course material.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our son has been a heroin addict for 10 years. He has drifted in and out of homelessness and prison. We have spent a fortune on rehab, counseling, legal fees and more, which changed nothing. We love our son, but we have also painfully learned that he is an inveterate liar, will steal anything not nailed down and has an incredible capacity for manipulation. We are in the process of updating our wills to reflect this reality, and we are struggling to articulate a fair plan as to inheritances. Our other children are living normal, happy, productive lives. Under other circumstances, we would split everything equally among our children, but for our addicted son, this would be like throwing gasoline on a fire. Even the process of distributing family treasures is fraught, knowing our son would head straight to a pawnshop. We are thinking of putting his share into a trust to be used exclusively for his future health needs, rehab and, hopefully, sobriety. But again, we love our son, and it is heartbreaking to realize we are essentially leaving him nothing tangible. What is the ethical thing for us to do? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11