ippet': 'The company, citing a human rights report it commissioned, said it was moving to prevent further abuses of its platform in Myanmar. Critics said they were unconvinced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Facebook has long promoted itself as a tool for bringing people together to make the world a better place. Now the social media giant has acknowledged that in Myanmar it did the opposite, and human rights groups say it has a lot of work to do to fix that.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/merlin_127193108_3968b053-8318-4cd6-8baf-e3dd44d2211e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/merlin_127193108_3968b053-8318-4cd6-8baf-e3dd44d2211e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/merlin_127193108_3968b053-8318-4cd6-8baf-e3dd44d2211e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/merlin_127193108_3968b053-8318-4cd6-8baf-e3dd44d2211e-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/07myanmar-facebook1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/07myanmar-facebook1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/07myanmar-facebook1/07myanmar-facebook1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook Admits It Was Used to Incite Violence in Myanmar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Facebook Admits Role Platform Had in Fueling Violence in Myanmar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rohingya (Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Social Responsibility', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Business for Social Responsibility', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-06T15:18:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b0152cf-932b-5267-8a95-f656cce44077', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b0152cf-932b-5267-8a95-f656cce44077'}, {'abstract': 'Actors of Asian descent have long either been cast as stereotypes — in embarrassing, demeaning roles — or simply whitewashed out of the picture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/06/t-magazine/asian-american-actors-representation.html', 'snippet': 'Actors of Asian descent have long either been cast as stereotypes — in embarrassing, demeaning roles — or simply whitewashed out of the picture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THEIR FACES ARE familiar for the feelings of horror and shame they produce in me. Mickey Rooney as Mr. Yunioshi in 1961’s “Breakfast at Tiffany’s,” stumbling around his Manhattan apartment in a blue bathrobe, his face contorted — lips barely closing over grotesquely pronounced buckteeth, slicked-back hair dyed jet black. Ashton Kutcher as a Bollywood producer, Raj, in a 2012 commercial, his skin darkened, a brown mustache affixed to his face, speaking in a cheap singsong voice, swaying his body, which is clad in a bright blue silk sherwani, back and forth to imitate the Indian head waggle. Tilda Swinton, otherworldly in her beauty, as always, but monkishly bald as the Ancient One, a character originally intended to be Tibetan, in 2016’s “Doctor Strange.” More subtle, but still just as shocking: Emma Stone — blonde and green-eyed — as Allison Ng, leaning against a kitchen island in a scene from 2015’s “Aloha” and saying, “My dad was half Chinese, half Hawaiian,” as breezily as if she were saying goodbye to someone on the telephone.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '60', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1373, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/06tmag-asianamerican-hp/06tmag-asianamerican-slide-KR8V-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Do Asian-Americans Remain Largely Unseen in Film and Television?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Look at Us', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-06T15:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thessaly La Force', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thessaly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Force', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08e74a9e-33d4-5b62-8d0f-fe4543d33f54', 'word_count': 3338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08e74a9e-33d4-5b62-8d0f-fe4543d33f54'}, {'abstract': 'These linen beauties — a collaboration between the apron maker Hedley & Bennett and the home goods company Parachute — add warmth to the Thanksgiving dinner.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/06/dining/linen-runners-hedley-and-bennett-parachute.html', 'snippet': 'These linen beauties — a collaboration between the apron maker Hedley & Bennett and the home goods company Parachute — add warmth to the Thanksgiving dinner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Skip the tablecloth, or even the place mats. These new linen runners, nearly 8-feet long (90 inches) and 16-inches wide, will smarten your holiday table the way a great belt or tie might enhance your outfit. They come in autumnal tones — “merlot” and “curry” — with contrasting edging, but if your tastes are more neutral, there’s also “oat.” And they won’t break the budget. The runners are a collaboration between Hedley & Bennett, which makes aprons and chef wear, and Parachute, a home goods company.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/dining/07burner6/merlin_146229963_c538396c-da97-4512-97e4-ae86506cb09d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/07/dining/07burner6/merlin_146229963_c538396c-da97-4512-97e4-ae86506cb09d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/dining/07burner6/merlin_1462