'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T06:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae755137-3d58-5718-818f-5856736d4226', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae755137-3d58-5718-818f-5856736d4226'}, {'abstract': 'O’Rourke gave Texans who have long felt disenfranchised a glimpse of what could be.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/opinion/swartz-beto-cruz-texas.html', 'snippet': 'O’Rourke gave Texans who have long felt disenfranchised a glimpse of what could be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — The condolence emails are already coming in, from New York, and California, and from my neighbors down the street, who are mostly limiting themselves to unprintable but predictable four-letter words. Yes, Beto O’Rourke lost to Ted Cruz, 51-48 percent, an outcome that surprised almost no seasoned politicos around these parts — they’ve been predicting it for months, some since the congressman from El Paso first announced his candidacy last year. Rest assured that the Monday-morning quarterbacking will proceed apace starting … now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/opinion/07swartzSub/merlin_146459310_e3d73bf0-ba30-4495-b306-15299cc2559f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/07/opinion/07swartzSub/merlin_146459310_e3d73bf0-ba30-4495-b306-15299cc2559f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/