ixed spot (even through your eyelids). Avoid the operatic gesture.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The secret is to have your mouth slightly open,” says Brian Blessed, 82, a British actor who played the Roman emperor Caesar Augustus in the 1976 BBC television series “I, Claudius.” In his much-lauded death scene, the camera stays in close on Blessed’s face as he slowly succumbs to poisoned figs and then remains cadaver-like for nearly three solid minutes. A slack mouth forces your body into a sleepy limpness and allows you to take tiny, imperceptible breaths. To be convincing, you must not visibly breathe, swallow, blink or let your eyes or body convey any of the twitchy, lit-from-within traits of the living. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Tip-1/11mag-Tip-1-jumbo.png', 'height': 500, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Tip-1/11mag-Tip-1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 500, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Tip-1/11mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Tip-1/11mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Tip-1/11mag-Tip-1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Play Dead', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Play Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af04075c-3cc7-5c6c-b7d2-88e92327d0e8', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af04075c-3cc7-5c6c-b7d2-88e92327d0e8'}, {'abstract': 'Google is helping relieve the knowledge workers of the world from the drudgery of email — by revealing how inhuman it was in the first place.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/magazine/you-already-email-like-a-robot-why-not-automate-it.html', 'snippet': 'Google is helping relieve the knowledge workers of the world from the drudgery of email — by revealing how inhuman it was in the first place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1996, Microsoft unleashed Clippit, better known as Clippy, on users of Microsoft Office. The legendarily irritating mascot-helper spent the following years hovering around the edges of documents, blinking dumbly under his lascivious eyebrows and blurting out, “It looks like you’re writing a letter,” until he was sidelined by the company in 2001, officially recognized as a mistake. Clippy’s problems were manifold. He announced his presence, via a personified avatar, to tell us something that we already knew (or that should have been obvious in the first place) and then proudly offered us little in the way of actual help. He sat and watched us and learned nothing, and repeated himself. He said too much and did too little.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 698, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 698}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 881, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1761, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ontech-image1/11mag-ontech-image1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'You Already Email Like a Robot — Why Not Automate It?', 'kicker': 'On Technology', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Herrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0687b2e6-3f9b-55ae-851d-499fd3b3b2fc', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0687b2e6-3f9b-55ae-851d-499fd3b3b2fc'}, {'abstract': 'New research is zeroing in on a biochemical basis for the placebo effect — possibly opening a Pandora’s box for Western medicine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/magazine/placebo-effect-medicine.html', 'snippet': 'New research is zeroing in on a biochemical basis for the placebo effect — possibly opening a Pandora’s box for Western medicine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chain of Office of the Dutch city of Leiden is a broad and colorful ceremonial necklace that, draped around the shoulders of Mayor Henri Lenferink, lends a magisterial air to official proceedings in this ancient university town. But whatever gravitas it provided Lenferink as he welcomed a group of researchers to his city, he was quick to undercut it. “I am just a humble historian,” he told the 300 members of the Society for Interdisciplinary Placebo Studies who had gathered in Leiden’s ornate municipal concert hall, “so I don’t know anything about your topic.” He was being a little disingenuous. He knew enough about the topic that these psychologists and neuroscientists and physicians and anthropologists and philosophers had come to his city to talk about — the placebo effect, the phenomenon whereby suffering people get better from treatments that have no discernible reason to work — to call it “fake medicine,” and to add that it probably works because “people like to be cheated.” He took a beat. “But in the end, I believe that honesty will prevail.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-articleLarge.png', 'height': 742, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 742}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 829, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1657, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-Placebo-image2/11mag-Placebo-image2-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What if the Placebo Effect Isn’t a Trick?', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Nothing Works', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Placebos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACUPUNCTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaptchuk, Ted J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26bc3c3a-1350-55fe-9220-323f2c1b857d', 'word_count': 5844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26bc3c3a-1350-55fe-9220-323f2c1b857d'}, {'abstract': 'With a new permanent New York shop, the online beauty brand that could explores a brave new world: offline.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/style/glossier-new-store-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'With a new permanent New York shop, the online beauty brand that could explores a brave new world: offline.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The clean, well-lighted place is glowing. Even from the sidewalk, even without windows you can see through. There it is on Lafayette Street, a salt lamp of retail real estate, a frosted beacon. There is a name-printed banner flying above, but if you are coming here, you probably don’t need it. You already know, deep in your pores, that you are at the new Glossier flagship store.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/merlin_146424135_9eed0bcd-9185-40b3-ba10-58ae7113e129-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/merlin_146424135_9eed0bcd-9185-40b3-ba10-58ae7113e129-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 777}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/merlin_146424135_9eed0bcd-9185-40b3-ba10-58ae7113e129-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/merlin_146424135_9eed0bcd-9185-40b3-ba10-58ae7113e129-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1583, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/08GLOSSIER-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/08GLOSSIER-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/fashion/08GLOSSIER-2/08GLOSSIER-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Glossier Will See You Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Human Touch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cosmetics and Toiletries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Glossier', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiss, Emily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-selfcare', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Schneier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45b87898-2123-5d4d-abfa-79e9f132b129', 'word_count': 683, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45b87898-2123-5d4d-abfa-79e9f132b129'}, {'abstract': 'I traveled to the proud home of basil pesto to learn the secrets of the sauce from a lifelong pounder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/magazine/pesto-italy-mortar-pestle.html', 'snippet': 'I traveled to the proud home of basil pesto to learn the secrets of the sauce from a lifelong pounder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throughout my time working in restaurants, I developed an illogical dread of some basic kitchen tasks. None of them — picking and chopping parsley, peeling and mincing garlic, browning pans of ground meat — were particularly difficult. But at the scale required in a professional kitchen, they felt Sisyphean. And even though it has been years since I worked in a restaurant and I now cook for one, two, sometimes maybe eight, rather than 100, the dread still plagues me. But once I get going, I am a goldfish, invariably surprised that chopping, browning or peeling for a handful of people takes barely any time.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-eat-slide-S92W/11mag-eat-slide-S92W-articleLarge.png', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/magazine/11mag-ea