0', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ed06527f-6292-59af-8cea-71ef17779bc2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ed06527f-6292-59af-8cea-71ef17779bc2'}, {'abstract': 'Nadia Olive Schnack’s Copenhagen home mixes unexpected, even clashing hues with an almost anarchic disrespect for convention. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/t-magazine/nadia-olive-schnack-scandinavian-designer-home.html', 'snippet': 'Nadia Olive Schnack’s Copenhagen home mixes unexpected, even clashing hues with an almost anarchic disrespect for convention. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'BY AGE 10, Nadia Olive Schnack had already come to regard the tasteful neutrality of Scandinavian interiors with disdain. Unbidden, the self-taught Danish designer, now 39, closed her bedroom door in her family’s house in the northern town of Aalborg to slather a magnificent 1920s-era built-in bookcase with what she calls “ugly purple paint.” It was the opening salvo in her war on the Nordic gray-and-white aesthetic — a joyful, full-spectrum battle royal that has reached its apotheosis in the Copenhagen house she shares with her husband and their two children.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '86', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T/07tmag-schnack-slide-XT0T-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Danish Designer Wages Battle Against Tasteful Nordic Grays', 'kicker': 'Home And Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Opposite of Neutral', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Copenhagen (Denmark)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T15:28:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gisela Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gisela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37223565-7410-54e9-bdb7-399d474cc0c6', 'word_count': 661, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37223565-7410-54e9-bdb7-399d474cc0c6'}, {'abstract': 'Making it easier to buy a weapon was once unthinkable in the country. But the interior minister, working from a populist playbook, is pushing a hard line.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/world/europe/italy-gun-laws-matteo-salvini.html', 'snippet': 'Making it easier to buy a weapon was once unthinkable in the country. But the interior minister, working from a populist playbook, is pushing a hard line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — At a police convention in October, Matteo Salvini, the most powerful figure in Italy’s populist government, ran his hands over an enormous military-grade sniper rifle and posed with a submachine gun. Before elections this year, he campaigned at a gun show and signed a cooperation pledge with a group advocating looser gun laws in Italy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/xxxitalyguns5/xxxitalyguns5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/07/world/xxxitalyguns5/xxxitalyguns5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/xxxitalyguns5/xxxitalyguns5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/07/world/xxxitalyguns5/xxxitalyguns5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u