of Marijuana Laws', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Dakota', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Utah', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-07T20:04:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1214e005-042d-5636-b0ff-481f4a2291e0', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1214e005-042d-5636-b0ff-481f4a2291e0'}, {'abstract': 'Maruja Mallo’s Surrealist works get a long-overdue survey; Neil Goldberg makes the personal political; and Martha Edelheit’s paintings and drawings of the sexually permissive ’60s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/07/arts/design/what-to-see-in-new-york-art-galleries-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Maruja Mallo’s Surrealist works get a long-overdue survey; Neil Goldberg makes the personal political; and Martha Edelheit’s paintings and drawings of the sexually permissive ’60s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maruja Mallo — whose given name was Ana María Gómez González — was an avant-garde painter living in Madrid in the 1920s and early ’30s, before fleeing to Argentina at the start of the Spanish Civil War. (She returned to Spain in 1965.) Mallo pursued Surrealism in the company of Salvador Dalí, the filmmaker Luis Buñuel and the poet Federico García Lorca, but as with many female artists, her contributions are only beginning to be studied.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non