c Opinion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-08T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas B. Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ccdd313-228c-5b25-b30a-043c41a0ded4', 'word_count': 2048, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ccdd313-228c-5b25-b30a-043c41a0ded4'}, {'abstract': '“The Time Is Now!” at the Smart Museum of Art in Chicago examines a watershed cultural moment and the African-American artists figures who defined it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/08/arts/design/smart-museum-chicago-black-artists.html', 'snippet': '“The Time Is Now!” at the Smart Museum of Art in Chicago examines a watershed cultural moment and the African-American artists figures who defined it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chicago — “Stop the murder of brothers and sisters.” “Liberate our nation.” “Unite.” The exhortations, on the vibrantly colored canvas of Gerald Williams’s “Messages,” couldn’t be more timely. Yet it is nearly 50 years old, made in 1970 during another politically fraught period in this country’s history.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/09/arts/09smartmuseum1/merlin_144776100_a59700e2-b67b-4644-8458-47ddc3c799a0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'ar