or': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Curacao', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-08T15:47:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Akwaeke Emezi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Akwaeke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emezi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b297bab-216f-51bc-805e-a98956f87be6', 'word_count': 1923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b297bab-216f-51bc-805e-a98956f87be6'}, {'abstract': 'Where to stay and what to see on this vibrant isle ringed with golden-sand beaches and pristine coral reefs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/08/t-magazine/curacao-travel-guide.html', 'snippet': 'Where to stay and what to see on this vibrant isle ringed with golden-sand beaches and pristine coral reefs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the southern Caribbean, just 40 miles off the coast of Venezuela and 12 degrees north of the Equator, the 40-mile-long, 8-mile-wide island of Curaçao represents a lively amalgam of different cultures. Part of the Lesser Antilles, also known as the ABC Islands — Aruba is to the west, Bonaire to the east — Curaçao was once a booming trade hub for merchants in the Dutch West India Company. Drawn to the island’s abundance of salt and its enclosed natural harbor, Schottegat, they settled on Curaçao in the mid-17th century and led the West African slave trade from its capital port city of Willemstad. Following the abolition of slavery in the 19th century, Curaçao fell into ruin but was later revived, in the 20th century, during a boom in Venezuela’s oil trade; in 2010, the country was granted autonomy by the dissolution of the Netherlands Antilles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/06/t-magazine/travel/Curacao-slide-02AR/Curacao-slide-02AR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Guide to Curaçao, a Laid-Back Caribbean Island With Candy-Colored Houses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seafood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Curacao', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-08T15:50:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michaela Trimble', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michaela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trimble', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28153d98-4dbd-5b95-acb2-a68607634a18', 'word_count': 1147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28153d98-4dbd-5b95-acb2-a68607634a18'}, {'abstract': 'When the F.C.C. backs telecommunications companies over local governments, low-income residents suffer the most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/08/opinion/low-income-san-jose-california-broadband.html', 'snippet': 'When the F.C.C. backs telecommunications companies over local governments, low-income residents suffer the most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 10.7 million low-income households in the United States lack access to quality internet service. In cities like San Jose, Calif., local governments are using streetlight poles to facilitate equitable access to high-speed internet to dramatically improve educational outcomes for low-income students and expand economic opportunity for their families. Unfortunately, a recent mandate by the Federal Communications Commission might halt the progress made by these cities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/opinion/08Liccardo2/08Liccardo2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name