e/25c8954e-fdf5-5268-bd63-e0a0cfd91293', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25c8954e-fdf5-5268-bd63-e0a0cfd91293'}, {'abstract': 'Thirty years ago, California became the first state to ban assault rifles. Here’s a look at its gun regulations, which have grown only stricter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/08/us/california-gun-laws.html', 'snippet': 'Thirty years ago, California became the first state to ban assault rifles. Here’s a look at its gun regulations, which have grown only stricter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'California, where a gunman killed 12 people in a bar in Thousand Oaks on Wednesday night, has some of the strictest gun laws in the country. It was the first state to ban assault weapons in 1989 after a shooting at a Stockton elementary school left five students dead. In the wake of several recent mass shootings — including one in February in Parkland, Fla., where a gunman killed 17 students and employees at a high school — state legislators put forward at least nine new gun control bills in response. Here’s a look at the state of gun regulations in California:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/09/us/09calgunreg/merlin_137864562_2607cc38-fa0b-4c66-8a24-cccc111c873c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/09/us/09calgunreg/merlin_137864562_2607cc38-fa0b-4c66-8a24-cccc111c873c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/09/us/09calgunreg/merlin_137864562_2607cc38-fa0b-4c66-8a24-cccc111c873c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/09/us/09cal