oured the world, often to record the aftermath of calamity, and collaborated on books with acclaimed writers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/08/obituaries/jean-mohr-dead.html', 'snippet': 'He toured the world, often to record the aftermath of calamity, and collaborated on books with acclaimed writers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jean Mohr, a Swiss photographer who brought a humanist’s eye to refugee camps, the Palestinian territories and places of distress all over the world, died on Nov. 3 in Geneva. He was 93.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/obituaries/11MOHR5/10MOHR6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jean Mohr, Photographer Who Found Heart Amid Bleakness, Dies at 93', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jean Mohr, Photographer and Humanitarian, Is Dead at 93', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohr, Jean (1925-2018)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-08T22:24:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Obits', 'section_name': 'Obituaries', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c6f9c84-448c-55c9-b08c-d52fbb0fd4f2', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c6f9c84-448c-55c9-b08c-d52fbb0fd4f2'}, {'abstract': 'The Hirshhorn Museum’s purchase of a piece by Tino Sehgal reveals a different kind of acquisition process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/08/t-magazine/tino-sehgal-hirshhorn-museum-art.html', 'snippet': 'The Hirshhorn Museum’s purchase of a piece by Tino Sehgal reveals a different kind of acquisition process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE ARTIST TINO SEHGAL believes there are enough objects in the world. His art involves people, not things, and the process of selling it is entirely verbal. Sehgal does not issue receipts. He does not permit his work to be photographed or filmed, nor does he produce catalogs or even wall labels to accompany his exhibitions. In fact, he eschews all forms of documentation, written or otherwise, in relation to the sale, presentation and care of his work. His pieces, which he calls “constructed situations,” usually involve “players” or “participants” who have been trained by the artist to enact specific actions. At the 2005 Venice Biennale, in a piece titled “This Is So Contemporary,” participants dressed as security guards leapt around visitors to the German Pavilion chanting, “Ooh, this is so contemporary, contemporary, contemporary!” before resuming nonchalant poses. For a 2010 show at the Guggenheim Museum called “This Progress,” a series of increasingly older interpreters guided visitors up the museum’s spiraled rotunda while carrying on free-form conversations that began with the visitor’s personal definition of progress. His most famous work, “Kiss,” from 2002, involves a man and a woman recreating iconic embraces from artworks throughout history, inspired by oeuvres as distinct as those of Auguste Rodin and Jeff Koons, on the floor of an exhibition space.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/t-magazine/08tmag-sehgal/08tmag-sehgal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/08/t-magazine/08tmag-sehgal/08tmag-sehgal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/t-magazine/08tmag-sehgal/08tmag-sehgal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/08/t-magazine/08tmag-sehgal/08tmag-sehgal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1535, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp