e, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7e1b72c-13a7-5559-84a6-d50f1d31b6b1', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7e1b72c-13a7-5559-84a6-d50f1d31b6b1'}, {'abstract': '“Tales From the Inner City,” by the acclaimed Shaun Tan, uses weird, striking images to tell stories about how we share the earth with creatures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/09/books/review/shaun-tan-tales-from-the-inner-city.html', 'snippet': '“Tales From the Inner City,” by the acclaimed Shaun Tan, uses weird, striking images to tell stories about how we share the earth with creatures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Shaun Tan won a Boston Globe-Horn Book Award in 2008 for “The Arrival,” the judges despaired of figuring out a category (picture book? fiction?) for its 128 wordless pages of sepia images. They awarded it a “special citation,” affirming its general weirdness and the genre-busting nature of its format, but mainly its remarkable, metaphor-made-concrete evocation of the stranger in a strange land, a man negotiating a new landscape, new people, new pets, all without the benefit of knowing the language.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11Sutton-01/merlin_145739316_be7201f5-ffc4-45bb-b9b1-fe20fe0798e1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11Sutton-01/merlin_145739316_be7201f5-ffc4-45bb-b9b1-fe20fe0798e1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11Sutton-01/merlin_145739316_be7201f5-ffc4-45bb-b9b1-fe20fe0798e1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType':