99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10a3da2c-e3f6-5e22-b441-4c3ba2d3ed2b'}, {'abstract': 'Why do critters feature in so many memorable kids books? Novels by Sharon Creech, Carl Hiaasen and more show once again how animals help us understand the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/09/books/review/patricia-maclachlan-my-fathers-words.html', 'snippet': 'Why do critters feature in so many memorable kids books? Novels by Sharon Creech, Carl Hiaasen and more show once again how animals help us understand the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So much of early childhood is about animals. We dress toddlers in T-shirts emblazoned with zoo creatures, teach them songs about livestock and tuck them in with stuffed bunnies. Their picture books feature peace-loving bulls, cookie-loving mice and oversize red dogs who make excellent stand-ins for little humans. But the middle-grade years — when kids’ passions are still more fixed on kittens and horses than the opposite sex — are the real sweet spot for animal books. This is when we get animal protagonists with inner lives as complicated as a 10-year-old’s (“Charlotte’s Web”) and tender stories about kids whose beloved pets help them grow and mature (I call them bildogsromans), like Kate DiCamillo’s “Because of Winn-Dixie.” There’s also the recurring theme of an animal rescued by a resourceful child — what could feel more empowering to a 10-year-old than saving a life? Four new novels provide their own twists on these classic story lines and prove, once again, that tales about animals can help kids understand the world and themselves.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11-BKS-Hong01/11-BKS-Hong01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 490, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11-BKS-Hong01/11-BKS-Hong01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 490, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11-BKS-Hong01/11-BKS-Hong01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11-BKS-Hong01/11-BKS-Hong01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/books/review/11-BKS-Hong01/11-BKS-Hong01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Great New Children’s Books About Animals', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Novels / The Wild Ones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "My Father's Words (Book)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacLachlan, Patricia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Saving Winslow (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Creech, Sharon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Squirm (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hiaasen, Carl', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Winnie's Great War (Book)", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mattick, Lindsay (Author)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenhut, Josh (Author)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blackall, Sophie', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '20