': 'Whites', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douthat, Ross G', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-10T19:42:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e63d8e6-0782-5cc1-b7ec-cebda2f57699', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e63d8e6-0782-5cc1-b7ec-cebda2f57699'}, {'abstract': 'It wasn’t going to bring him back, or keep others safe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/10/opinion/sunday/shooting-victims-guns.html', 'snippet': 'It wasn’t going to bring him back, or keep others safe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1992, my son was killed in a school shooting in Massachusetts, a random victim of a disturbed fellow college student who’d purchased a semiautomatic rifle at a local gun shop and smuggled it onto campus. College officials had been warned that this student had a gun, but they didn’t know how to respond; school shootings were still too new. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/11/opinion/11gibson/merlin_145888215_fdb96585-3516-435f-a3e4-19eed4adb82c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/11/opinion/11gibson/merlin_145888215_fdb96585-3516-435f-a3e4-19eed4adb82c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype