Express', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ae3ce7c-ca74-5751-a514-e0a6c7be8d68', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ae3ce7c-ca74-5751-a514-e0a6c7be8d68'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Sanders, a veteran of four decades of stage and screen work, is giving the performance of his career in his first appearance in Chekhov.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/11/theater/jay-o-sanders-uncle-vanya.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Sanders, a veteran of four decades of stage and screen work, is giving the performance of his career in his first appearance in Chekhov.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jay O. Sanders is, by his own definition, “an oak tree” — an imposing physical type associated with sturdiness, reliability and, on occasion, menace. And because Mr. Sanders has been a much-employed actor on stage and screen for some 40 years, he knows that oaks are rarely cast as the neurotic and cerebral title character of Chekhov’s “Uncle Vanya.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/merlin_146581662_c60a44d1-1e57-44d1-9800-d6733f52a1c3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/merlin_146581662_c60a44d1-1e57-44d1-9800-d6733f52a1c3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/merlin_146581662_c60a44d1-1e57-44d1-9800-d6733f52a1c3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/merlin_146581662_c60a44d1-1e57-44d1-9800-d6733f52a1c3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1639, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/13sanders2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/13sanders2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/arts/13sanders2/13sanders2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jay O. Sanders on How an ‘Oak Tree’ Became Uncle Vanya', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An ‘Oak Tree’ As a Perfect Uncle Vanya', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hunter College', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Richard J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chekhov, Anton', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Jay O', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Uncle Vanya (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-11T21:42:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80d9bf88-d26b-568b-a491-f2239ba14442', 'word_count': 1156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80d9bf88-d26b-568b-a491-f2239ba14442'}, {'abstract': 'Manchester City’s stunning start to the season is perhaps best appreciated by the idea that its pursuers have been nearly flawless — and can’t keep up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/11/sports/manchester-city-united-derby.html', 'snippet': 'Manchester City’s stunning start to the season is perhaps best appreciated by the idea that its pursuers have been nearly flawless — and can’t keep up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, England — Leroy Sané took a touch, and flicked the ball to Raheem Sterling. Ashley Young, the Manchester United captain, tracked the pass. Sterling controlled the ball and slipped it back to Sané. Young shuttled back the way he came. Sané waited until he arrived, and then rolled the ball to Phil Foden. Young set off again. Foden back to Sané, Sané to Sterling, around and around, again and again, hypnotic and unstoppable and relentless.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/12/sports/soccer/12onsoccer1-print/merlin_146689995_45613dda-432c-4a48-b68d-057171369745-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/12/sports/soccer/12onsoccer1-print/merlin_146689995_45613dda-432c-4a48-b68d-057171369745-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/12/sports/soccer/12onsoccer1-print/merlin_146689995_45613dda-432c-4a48-b68d-057171369745-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 766, 'wid