ywright a decade after his death is both exhilarating and exhausting in its urgency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/13/opinion/culture/harold-pinter-death-play.html', 'snippet': 'A monthslong celebration of the playwright a decade after his death is both exhilarating and exhausting in its urgency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Ten years after Harold Pinter’s death, it almost feels as if we’re trapped in one of his furrowed-brow-inducing plays. What is real? What is untrue? Why is everyone so creepy and annoying?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/10/opinion/10pinter/10pinter-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why It Feels Like We’re Trapped in a Pinter Play', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinter, Harold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-13T11:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Opinion | Culture', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Belcher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belcher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fe601b4-cd1c-5d71-b8a4-9f41f4f7263c', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fe601b4-cd1c-5d71-b8a4-9f41f4f7263c'}, {'abstract': 'She’s adored in part because she’s always been transparent about her life’s tensions. Her memoir is just the latest example.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/13/opinion/michelle-obama-book-becoming.html', 'snippet': 'She’s adored in part because she’s always been transparent about her life’s tensions. Her memoir is just the latest example.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few days before the release of Michelle Obama’s memoir, “Becoming,” I went to look at her portrait at the National Portrait Gallery to reflect on her evolving role in public life, and to talk to other women who were doing the same.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/opinion/13brownWeb/13brownWeb-articleLa