f-b8fd-e659052f7efe', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72afee36-686f-51ef-b8fd-e659052f7efe'}, {'abstract': '“Aunty!,” an exhibit curated by Laylah Amatullah Barrayn and Catherine E. McKinley, reveals photography’s role as a tool or weapon when investigating identity and empowerment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/14/lens/photography-african-women.html', 'snippet': '“Aunty!,” an exhibit curated by Laylah Amatullah Barrayn and Catherine E. McKinley, reveals photography’s role as a tool or weapon when investigating identity and empowerment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uncovering the beauty of African women and their worlds within fractured and layered colonial histories demands a special attention to detail. As the documentary photographer Laylah Amatullah Barrayn and the author Catherine E. McKinley know, many of the earliest images weren’t meant to be seen as art. Yet they had a disturbing, global reach, fueled by the racism and voyeurism of unknown European photographers. And they trigger discomfort — if not outrage — to this day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/16/lens/14aunty2/14aunty2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/16/lens/14aunty2/14aunty2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 610}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/16/lens/14aunty2/14aunty2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1008, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/16/lens/14aunty2/14aunty2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2016, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': Non