es', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Education Assn', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-14T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/069a2c65-8971-508d-a3db-6eb0a2d6fe08', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/069a2c65-8971-508d-a3db-6eb0a2d6fe08'}, {'abstract': 'We changed queer literature, and the world, by writing our own stories. With disability, we can do it again. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/14/opinion/telling-new-disability-stories.html', 'snippet': 'We changed queer literature, and the world, by writing our own stories. With disability, we can do it again. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Story creates culture. It teaches us to feel, think and behave in ways generally approved of by those around us. Story conditions us. This conditioning, or bias, is not always visible to us, but it constrains and guides our behavior. Implicit bias can’t be changed until we learn to see the old, embedded story and then find a new and better story to overwrite it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/merlin_146756022_6e1bfce5-9e90-426f-83fa-fa2e0591eff8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/merlin_146756022_6e1bfce5-9e90-426f-83fa-fa2e0591eff8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/merlin_146756022_6e1bfce5-9e90-426f-83fa-fa2e0591eff8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/merlin_146756022_6e1bfce5-9e90-426f-83fa-fa2e0591eff8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/14disability-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/14disability-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/opinion/14disability/14disability-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rewriting the Old Disability Script', 'kicker': 'disability', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-14T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicola Griffith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicola', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Griffith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/cadb8035-0742-5665-86bb-704988e64c2d', 'word_count': 1174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cadb8035-0742-5665-86bb-704988e64c2d'}, {'abstract': 'Predictable vacations are so, well, predictable. Here are five tips on how to increase your chances of unexpected encounters and surprising moments. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/14/travel/how-to-make-travel-less-predictable.html', 'snippet': 'Predictable vacations are so, well, predictable. Here are five tips on how to increase your chances of unexpected encounters and surprising moments. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing against relaxing on beachfront daybeds or doing the cathedral-monument-museum circuit, but for travelers like me (and, I’m guessing, like some of you) the ideal vacation is one that combines relaxation and sightseeing with unexpected finds down narrow streets, lively conversations with surprising locals and wrong exits leading to hidden treasures.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/03/10/travel/10trending/merlin_146522427_66786150-dd06-4b88-b490-0f3a5a8ec837-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/03/10/travel/10trending/merlin_146522427_66786150-dd06-4b88-b490-0f3a5a8ec837-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/03/10/travel/10trending/merlin_146522427_66786150-dd06-4b88-b490-0f3a5a8ec837-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 6