tname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75714596-ef4d-58ea-8929-a2b30ef54fc2', 'word_count': 1061, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75714596-ef4d-58ea-8929-a2b30ef54fc2'}, {'abstract': "The comics writer's new Wonder Woman arc asks if war, and the violence it begets, can ever be just.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/14/books/wonder-woman-g-willow-wilson.html', 'snippet': "The comics writer's new Wonder Woman arc asks if war, and the violence it begets, can ever be just.", 'lead_paragraph': 'In the opening pages of “Wonder Woman” No. 58, Ares, the god of war, sits imprisoned in a dark cell. He has had an epiphany. It isn’t glorious conquest or love that accords immortality to the honorable. It is justice. It’s a perversely compelling moment — war personified, becoming a crusader for righteousness. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/books/14wilson1/merlin_146500011_47856cb9-d5b5-4024-9a91-b094da771532-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/14/books/14wilson1/merlin_146500011_47856cb9-d5b5-4024-9a91-b094da771532-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/books/14wilson1/merlin_146500011_47856cb9-d5b5-4024-9a91-b094da771532-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/books/14wilson1/merlin_146500011_47856cb9-d5b5-4024-9a91-b094da771532-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'su