ubsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Cyran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cyran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b34ad1aa-2ce5-57cc-bbd5-abab8c96b563', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b34ad1aa-2ce5-57cc-bbd5-abab8c96b563'}, {'abstract': 'The father of one of the American soldiers killed in Niger last year writes a letter of support for the Army captain who led the fateful mission.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/14/magazine/a-letter-to-the-army-dont-punish-the-man-who-led-the-mission-that-killed-my-son.html', 'snippet': 'The father of one of the American soldiers killed in Niger last year writes a letter of support for the Army captain who led the fateful mission.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After multiple investigations into the ambush in Niger that left four American soldiers dead last year, the United States Army punished several people who were involved in the mission, including the two soldiers who led the 11-man team when militants attacked it. Those punished were not reprimanded for what happened during the ambush but rather what led up to it, including poor training alongside Nigerien troops, a lack of pre-mission rehearsals and what investigators called a misleading mission plan. The leader of the Special Forces team, Capt. Mike Perozeni, along with his second in command, received a General Officer Memorandum of Reprimand, known as a GOMOR. A reprimand’s impact is based on where it is placed in a soldier’s file. If the letter is considered “local,” it will disappear after the soldier changes jobs. If it is permanent, it likely means the end of a career. The soldier, and others on his behalf, can write a rebuttal. Below is a letter written on behalf of Captain Perozeni by Henry Black, the father of Staff Sgt. Bryan Black, one of the four soldiers killed in the ambush. A version of this letter was delivered to Maj. Gen. Edwin J. Deedrick Jr., the commanding officer of the First Special Forces Command, who administered the letters. With Black’s permission, it has been lightly edited for clarity and to protect the identity of some of the soldiers, whose names have not been released. Thomas Gibbons-Neff', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/14/magazine/14atwar-letter/merlin_138667059_e98c9084-1e61-47f1-962b-d90d189e4f78-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Letter to the Army: Don’t Punish the Man Who Led the Mission That Killed My Son', 'kicker': 'AT WAR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Africa Command', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perozeni, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Niger', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-14T16:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b68cd5be-2d28-5661-aee7-aa88909885c4', 'word_count': 1187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b68cd5be-2d28-5661-aee7-aa88909885c4'}, {'abstract': 'A subtly accentuated pout can be achieved with makeup alone. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/14/style/bigger-lips-how-to.html', 'snippet': 'A subtly accentuated pout can be achieved with makeup alone. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fleshy, pouty, pillowy lips. Everywhere you turn, a new actress, model or influencer is pursing an outrageously plump pair of lips. If you were pointing fingers, you could blame the return of 1990s fashion and the beauty trends of that girlie-grunge decade. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/15/fashion/15SKINDEEP-COMBO/15SKINDEEP-COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 244, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'ar