'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f020731-4d48-5014-ac41-414dece84b64', 'word_count': 1808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f020731-4d48-5014-ac41-414dece84b64'}, {'abstract': 'Wall Street has dominated New York’s economy for too long. Amazon’s HQ2 is a huge opportunity to diversify.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/16/opinion/amazon-hq2-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Wall Street has dominated New York’s economy for too long. Amazon’s HQ2 is a huge opportunity to diversify.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades, experts have called for the diversification of the New York City economy. In the wake of postwar deindustrialization, our reliance on Wall Street and the financial industry in general had by the 1990s left our city disproportionately vulnerable to swings in the financial markets. We have experienced significant downturns that today feel like distant memories but could certainly return. I know this from my own experience as director of planning for Manhattan after Sept. 11. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/16/opinion/16CHAKRABARTI/16CHAKRABARTI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/16/opinion/16CHAKRABARTI/16CHAKRABARTI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/16/opinion/16CHAKRABARTI/16CHAKRABARTI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/16/opinion/16CHAKRABARTI/16CHAKRABARTI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1308, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'su