oser-performer who was all but lost to history after his death in 1990 — have become reliably important events as his music has resurfaced in recent years.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/merlin_146934969_b54cbabc-f7bc-44ce-b142-af95d00cc47c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 457, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/merlin_146934969_b54cbabc-f7bc-44ce-b142-af95d00cc47c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 457}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/merlin_146934969_b54cbabc-f7bc-44ce-b142-af95d00cc47c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/merlin_146934969_b54cbabc-f7bc-44ce-b142-af95d00cc47c-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/17eastman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/17eastman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/17/arts/music/17eastman/17eastman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Long-Lost Symphonic Love Story Is Told Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Symphonic Love Story Is Told Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New School', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eastman, Julius (1940- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MANNES ORCHESTRA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chessa, Luciano', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-16T18:24:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Colter Walls', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': 'Colter', 'lastname': 'Walls', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6f831fd-7ced-5ee4-8ad4-7bf530896baf', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6f831fd-7ced-5ee4-8ad4-7bf530896baf'}, {'abstract': 'Now that my son can read, I want him to seek out books about more than just winning things and getting easy laughs. Why does that seem hard?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/16/books/review/beyond-gender-stereotypes-boys.html', 'snippet': 'Now that my son can read, I want him to seek out books about more than just winning things and getting easy laughs. Why does that seem hard?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of my son’s favorite books last year was Cece Bell’s graphic novel “El Deafo,” an emotionally complex story about a girl contending with hearing loss, a new school and the trap of a bad friendship. Several boys in his second-grade class also found “El Deafo” a rewarding change from the usual fare recommended to them — those ubiquitous series that deliver endless action and flatulence jokes. For my son and his friends, it didn’t matter that the narrator of “El Deafo” is a girl, or that there is no galactic warfare to be found, no plotline about defeating others. Seeing them connect so strongly with this nuanced, absorbing novel made me wonder why boys at this age — the “chapter book” years, when they’re reading on their own, but not full-on novels — are pushed mostly toward shallow books full of battles, boogers and pranks.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18Novey/18Novey-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond Battles, Boogers and Pranks', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Children’s Books / Essay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Cece', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palacio, R J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-16T18:25:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Idra Novey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Idra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Novey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b446d964-c4e7-5257-be64-c4c4bd8e3a9f', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b446d964-c4e7-5257-be64-c4c4bd8e3a9f'}, {'abstract': 'In this excerpt from the introduction to a new edition of Saul Steinberg’s 1960 book, “The Labyrinth,” the novelist celebrates the artist as a “twirler of nonverbal non sequiturs.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/16/books/review/saul-steinberg-labyrinth.html', 'snippet': 'In this excerpt from the introduction to a new edition of Saul Steinberg’s 1960 book, “The Labyrinth,” the novelist celebrates the artist as a “twirler of nonverbal non sequiturs.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is fear as well as joy in Saul Steinberg’s travelogue. To make progress, you must enter the maze of what is possible, the maze of everywhere you’ve already gone and everywhere you might go. Start at Point A, and try to follow the path he has drawn. Your eyes leap up, and turn left, and then there’s a sort of guitar fretwork that you must navigate, which seems to have been made with the help of a ruler. Then comes a wiggly bit. Followed by another left turn, a sharp left, and then a hard right. Very soon the eye gives up, overwhelmed by distractions. The lines transgress and bisect themselves. Consciousness is too complicated. Point B should be attainable, but it isn’t.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/merlin_146102562_fcbb57a7-3e10-458e-a3e3-fe6f93e1e2bd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/merlin_146102562_fcbb57a7-3e10-458e-a3e3-fe6f93e1e2bd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 543}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/merlin_146102562_fcbb57a7-3e10-458e-a3e3-fe6f93e1e2bd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 926, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/merlin_146102562_fcbb57a7-3e10-458e-a3e3-fe6f93e1e2bd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1853, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/18BackPage-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/18BackPage-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/18/books/review/18BackPage-01/18BackPage-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 