 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of Legal Counsel (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blumenthal, Richard', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirono, Mazie K', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitaker, Matthew G', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitehouse, Sheldon', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-19T19:28:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage and Nicholas Fandos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fandos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8ea1954-2136-54b8-ba98-c9e7af9c016c', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8ea1954-2136-54b8-ba98-c9e7af9c016c'}, {'abstract': 'Washi is to the Japanese something like what wine is to the French — a national obsession and point of pride.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/19/t-magazine/japanese-paper-washi.html', 'snippet': 'Washi is to the Japanese something like what wine is to the French — a national obsession and point of pride.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE OF THE CLICHÉS of modernity — but a cliché we nonetheless have to live through — is that new forms of technology make us nostalgic for prior ones and the eras they connote. When smartphones emerged, they brought the internet into spaces that were once free of them, so that a poorly functioning flip phone now inspires a hint of wistfulness. The pileup of digitized music since the emergence of the MP3 has prompted a retreat, however niche and ultimately minor, into the world of vinyl records and even tapes. The ransacking of the physical texture of the world — books, newspapers, retail stores, maps — has been so enormous and sudden that it becomes possible to see what we are losing and no longer regard the onrushing future as progress.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 746, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1491, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/13/t-magazine/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO/tktmag-paper-slide-SQBO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Is Japan Still So Attached to Paper?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stationery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ozu, Yasujiro', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-19T19:31:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikil Saval', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saval', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b3bd2e7-9f14-51c9-a33e-bda0d1f53868', 'word_count': 1790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b3bd2e7-9f14-51c9-a33e-bda0d1f53868'}, {'abstract': 'John C. Reilly and Sarah Silverman return to voice the characters in this social satire sequel that is downright scary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/19/movies/ralph-breaks-the-internet-review.html', 'snippet': 'John C. Reilly and Sarah Silverman return to voice the characters in this social satire sequel that is downright scary.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Ralph Breaks the Internet” might look like just another adorable, funny animated family film, but it also connects to our current reality in ways that are downright bone-chilling. This sequel to the 2012 Disney hit “Wreck-It Ralph” — which was set in the virtual world of arcade games, and whose affable lug of a hero was an 8-bit video game villain trying to break free from a lifetime of mindless destruction — sends its protagonists out into the broader internet, where they discover all the pandering, cruelty, addictive behavior and viral shamelessness that we’ve come to associate with online culture.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 251}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/21/arts/21ralphbreaks/21ralphbreaks-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ralph Breaks the Internet’ Review: Disney Gets Ca