icker': 'letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sketches From California ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paradise (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wildfires', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-19T19:51:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Letters', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e77f8d10-dcf2-54c0-b253-516923abbbc8', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e77f8d10-dcf2-54c0-b253-516923abbbc8'}, {'abstract': 'Phyllida Barlow and Anna Maria Maiolino, who have both received full recognition for their work late in life, sit down for a conversation about their careers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/19/t-magazine/phyllida-barlow-anna-maria-maiolino-conversation-art.html', 'snippet': 'Phyllida Barlow and Anna Maria Maiolino, who have both received full recognition for their work late in life, sit down for a conversation about their careers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The artists Phyllida Barlow and Anna Maria Maiolino grew up continents apart, but they share a language of mud and cement, life cycles and rebirths. Barlow, 74, is a British artist renowned for her often-soaring sculptures cobbled together from industrial refuse, as though an ordinary urban street had suddenly come alive and risen up into a sentient colossus. Maiolino, 76, is an Italian, São Paulo-based artist celebrated for her modeled- and rolled-clay installations and sculptures that suggest the work of “a housewife gone mad,” as the curator Helen Molesworth once put it, and evoke everything from human organs, phalluses and rolls of bread to prehistoric worms. Barlow had a traditional arts education at London’s Chelsea College of Arts, followed by the Slade. Maiolino had a very nontraditional upbringing and education, migrating with her family from Italy to Venezuela during World War II, and then from Venezuela to Brazil, where her performances, videos and works on paper contributed, alongside work by Lygia Clark and Hélio Oiticica, to the Neo-Concrete and New Objectivity movements at a time of political upheaval in Brazil. (She audited classes at an art school in Rio de Janeiro but considers herself largely self-taught.) Both artists are mothers, and both received full recognition for their pioneering work late in life — and continue to make work in their 70s as a matter of some urgency. This month, their artworks are on view in concurrent solo exhibitions at Hauser & Wirth in New York, where we met on a brisk fall morning. There, the two artists sat down together to talk about their work and lives for the first time.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/t-magazine/art/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI/phllida-annamaria-slide-SUJI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Pioneering Artists Discuss Motherhood and Machismo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Conversation; Art, Politics, Motherhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hauser & Wirth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maiolino, Anna Maria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-19T19:59:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tess Thackara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thackara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f6614eb-d947-5471-bf27-540611bef5a7', 'word_count': 2389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f6614eb-d947-5471-bf27-540611bef5a7'}, {'abstract': 'It isn’t just size. Unlike many big American companies, Amazon is not squandering its profits on stock buybacks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2