 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pam Belluck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belluck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73a74e81-55cd-5806-95f3-6d2a0cb69e94', 'word_count': 1096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73a74e81-55cd-5806-95f3-6d2a0cb69e94'}, {'abstract': 'Biology was supposed to cure what ails psychiatry. Decades later, millions of people with mental disorders are still waiting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/19/health/mental-health-psychology.html', 'snippet': 'Biology was supposed to cure what ails psychiatry. Decades later, millions of people with mental disorders are still waiting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing humbles history’s great thinkers more quickly than reading their declarations on the causes of madness. Over the centuries, mental illness has been attributed to everything from a “badness of spirit” (Aristotle) and a “humoral imbalance” (Galen) to autoerotic fixation (Freud) and the weakness of the hierarchical state of the ego (Jung).', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/20/science/20SCI-QUESTIONS-MENTAL/merlin_146934624_86673202-8c94-4780-aa3e-471e0be7a01a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/20/science/20SCI-QUESTIONS-MENTAL/merlin_146934624_86673202-8c94-4780-aa3e-471e0be7a01a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/20/science/20SCI-QUESTIONS-MENTAL/merlin_146934624_86673202-8c94-4780-aa3e-471e0be7a01a-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'ju