, 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do Sneakerheads Wear at Thanksgiving?', 'kicker': 'Styles Investigates …', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Day for a Feast, but Probably Not the Hypebeast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-20T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Engel Bromwich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': 'Engel', 'lastname': 'Bromwich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90a8ca90-b4f8-56e7-b5b1-e3551cd8414c', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90a8ca90-b4f8-56e7-b5b1-e3551cd8414c'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on believing a friend is innocent of rape and assessing the role of grandparents in their grandchildren’s vaccinations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/20/magazine/am-i-wrong-to-believe-my-friend-is-innocent-of-rape.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on believing a friend is innocent of rape and assessing the role of grandparents in their grandchildren’s vaccinations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of my closest friends was accused of raping a fellow student in college. There were no criminal proceedings, and he has always maintained his innocence. In the years since, he has not been accused again. I know nothing about the woman who accused him other than what my friend has told me about her — or about what happened, other than what he has told me — and having not been in the room with them, I must live with the fact that the truth is in some respects unknowable. I have chosen to believe him, largely because I do not think raping someone is in his character, but I am also aware that, statistically, false allegations of rape are rare. With this in mind, is it ethically wrong to choose to believe my friend? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1300, 'width': 1300, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Am I Wrong 