': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richards, Jeffrey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-20T16:58:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Obits', 'section_name': 'Obituaries', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/9225a16a-97e8-5416-a715-8a16980f9bc1', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9225a16a-97e8-5416-a715-8a16980f9bc1'}, {'abstract': 'Could nasal breathing be more helpful for our brains than breathing through the mouth?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/20/well/mind/breathing-through-the-nose-may-offer-unique-brain-benefits.html', 'snippet': 'Could nasal breathing be more helpful for our brains than breathing through the mouth?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Folklore, spiritual traditions and even mothers have for ages drawn an implicit connection between respiration and state of mind: Breathe in deeply through your nose, we are told, to clarify thoughts, achieve serenity, defuse tantrums. There isn’t a lot of scientific evidence to back up these ideas, but a growing number of experiments have been looking at the influence that breathing has on our cognition. In October, a study in The Journal of Neuroscience considered the relationship between memory and how we breathe.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25Mag-Well-1/25Mag-Well-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25Mag-Well-1/25Mag-Well-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capt