man', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/669b4608-7b7b-59e7-b2d0-ea984a3516ce', 'word_count': 832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/669b4608-7b7b-59e7-b2d0-ea984a3516ce'}, {'abstract': 'The ancient Greek city of Tenea gives up its long-buried secrets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/22/opinion/a-city-lost-and-found.html', 'snippet': 'The ancient Greek city of Tenea gives up its long-buried secrets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Atlantis, El Dorado, Kitezh, Great Zimbabwe, Troy, the Lost City of the Incas. The legends of vanished cities, mythical or real, are an endless source of fascination and fantasy. For an archaeologist it is surely the ultimate ambition to one day burrow under some remote hummock and uncover the remnants of one such long-lost settlement, to reconstruct the shards, stones, bones and baubles into a place where people worked, argued, laughed, lived and died. And with every such discovery we all learn a bit more about who we are and where we come from.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 276, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 276}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 944, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22greece-edt/22greece-edt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A City Lost and Found', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A City Lost and Found', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greek Civilization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY AND ANTHROPOLOGY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tenea (Greece)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-23T00:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a49f42cb-2407-55b9-a62d-923a2d356c38', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a49f42cb-2407-55b9-a62d-923a2d356c38'}, {'abstract': 'Who should run the country next?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/22/opinion/populism-establishment-working-class.html', 'snippet': 'Who should run the country next?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What should we do during the next two years of divided government? We could spend them as we