ocations', 'value': 'Bangladesh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aung San Suu Kyi', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-23T00:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc23712e-8733-5b6f-b29f-9dbf7303edc1', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc23712e-8733-5b6f-b29f-9dbf7303edc1'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time to stop questioning whether women should be in combat units. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/22/opinion/let-women-be-warriors.html', 'snippet': 'It’s time to stop questioning whether women should be in combat units. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the nearly three years since the Pentagon allowed women to join front-line combat units, questions about the policy have not gone away. Two former Marines — Owen West, now an assistant secretary of defense, and his father, the military writer Bing West — said women in the infantry would “swiftly reduce combat effectiveness.” In The American Conservative, Scott Beauchamp could see no benefit in bringing women into combat roles and suggested that the Pentagon was cynically trying to bolster recruiting during ill-conceived wars. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/22/opinion/22fazio/merlin_122433431_186af553-dae4-4c7e-a1cf-92cfd86e5d87-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'lega