t', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-24T19:23:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacy Cowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e20eb032-7633-5308-87dd-ffe965799bf1', 'word_count': 1187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e20eb032-7633-5308-87dd-ffe965799bf1'}, {'abstract': 'The constant pressure to sell ourselves on every possible platform has produced its own brand of modern anxiety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/24/opinion/sunday/gig-economy-self-promotion-anxiety.html', 'snippet': 'The constant pressure to sell ourselves on every possible platform has produced its own brand of modern anxiety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something about the consumer madness of the holiday season that makes me think of my friend Rebecca’s mother. When I was in middle school, she had a side hustle selling acrylic-rhinestone bug brooches. The jewelry was hard to move on its merits — even for the 1980s it was staggeringly ugly. But what she lacked in salable product, she made up for in sheer selling stamina. Every sleepover, school fair or birthday party, out would come the tray of bejeweled grasshoppers and stag beetles, glinting with Reagan-era menace. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 656}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1872, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/26/opinion/sunday/25whippman/25whippman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Everything Is for Sale Now. Even Us. ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'We’re All in Sales Now', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freelancing, Self-Employment and Independent Contracting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-24T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth Whippman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whippman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/299858c8-0b76-5d5b-8499-2451db128e82', 'word_count': 1455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/299858c8-0b76-5d5b-8499-2451db128e82'}, {'abstract': 'The platform has been used to disrupt elections, disseminate propaganda and promote hate. Regular users should ask if they are implicated in these failings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/24/opinion/sunday/facebook-immoral.html', 'snippet': 'The platform has been used to disrupt elections, disseminate propaganda and promote hate. Regular users should ask if they are implicated in these failings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I joined Facebook in 2008, and for the most part, I have benefited from being on it. Lately, however, I have wondered whether I should delete my Facebook account. As a philosopher with a special interest in ethics, I am using “should” in the moral sense. That is, in light of recent events implicating Facebook in objectionable behavior, is there a duty to leave it?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/merlin_147226563_281cf082-7688-426d-8771-d2bf1d22583f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/merlin_147226563_281cf082-7688-426d-8771-d2bf1d22583f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/merlin_147226563_281cf082-7688-426d-8771-d2bf1d22583f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/merlin_147226563_281cf082-7688-426d-8771-d2bf1d22583f-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1322, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/25liao-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/25liao-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/sunday/25liao/25liao-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Have a Moral Duty to Leave Facebook?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do You Have a Moral Duty to Leave Facebook?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics and Official Misconduct', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-24T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By S. Matthew Liao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'Matthew', 'lastname': 'Liao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bd8dbdc-c267-5abf-8e8a-eee08de28960', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bd8dbdc-c267-5abf-8e8a-eee08de28960'}, {'abstract': 'A fable about Italy, bond yields and politics in the European Union.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/24/opinion/sunday/italy-europe-bond-spread.html', 'snippet': 'A fable about Italy, bond yields and politics in the European Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 2012, in the small town of San Zeno di Montagna in northern Italy, my grandfather welcomed a new addition to the family: One of his donkeys gave birth to a female foal, which he kept as a pet. Much to our amusement, he decided to call it Angela Spread. Angela, of course, was Angela Merkel, whose rigid approach to Europe’s fiscal crisis had become particularly infamous over the previous year. “Spread” referred to a somewhat obscure term in bond finance: the difference between the yields of Italian and German government bonds. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/25RUBINELLI02/merlin_141681597_a6e38b5b-8613-4af7-bd27-72c3a910ad94-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/25RUBINELLI02/merlin_141681597_a6e38b5b-8613-4af7-bd27-72c3a910ad94-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/25RUBINELLI02/merlin_141681597_a6e38b5b-8613-4af7-bd27-72c3a910ad94-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/opinion/25RUBINELL