m/2018/11/24/magazine/poem-parents.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Rita Dove.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The deceased parents in Ted Kooser’s poem are nothing like the cantankerous apparition of folklore, nor is their son “spooked” by their lingering presence. This was once their home, too, and their persistent housecleaning is one aspect of what they’ve always done — namely, look after their child. Aided by Kooser’s artful lineation, we are whisked from room to room, the quick in pursuit of the dead. A benign haunting, if you will: Perhaps the best way to keep the spirits of loved ones alive is to allow them to continue living within us. Selected by Rita Dove', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25mag-poem-image1/25mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 549, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25mag-poem-image1/25mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 549}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25mag-poem-image1/25mag-poem-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25mag-poem-image1/25mag-poem-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25mag-poem-image1/25mag-poem-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/25/magazine/25mag-poem-imag