', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f98d8859-a65a-5c96-9ba1-5d27131f0410', 'word_count': 1336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f98d8859-a65a-5c96-9ba1-5d27131f0410'}, {'abstract': 'Will the growing interest in traditional Chinese medicine — at home and abroad — doom some of the world’s rarest species?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/27/magazine/chinas-mixed-messages-on-the-global-trade-in-endangered-animal-parts.html', 'snippet': 'Will the growing interest in traditional Chinese medicine — at home and abroad — doom some of the world’s rarest species?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cryptic declaration from Beijing alarmed and mystified wildlife conservationists around the world. Reversing a 25-year-old ban on the trade of tiger bones and rhinoceros horns, the Chinese government announced in October that it would foster a “controlled” legal market in these goods. The move was unexpected, given that less than a year ago China took a major step in the fight against wildlife trafficking by doing precisely the opposite: banning the domestic sale of elephant ivory. Exotic-animal parts have become status symbols in parts of newly affluent China, but the demand for rhino horns and tiger bones is also driven by an ancient belief in their power to cure everything from fever to impotence.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/magazine/02OnMoney/02OnMoney-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Mixed Messages on the Global Trade in Endangered-Animal Parts', 'kicker': 'On Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China’s Mixed Messages on the Global Trade in Endangered-Animal Parts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poaching (Wildlife)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elephants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rhinoceroses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tigers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bones', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ivory', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WildAid', 'rank': 10, 'maj