, 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/27/opinion/betsy-devos-title-ix-schools-students.html', 'snippet': 'Institutions have been pushing for less liability when it comes to sexual misconduct on campus. Now they’re finally getting it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much of the coverage of proposed new rules released last week on how schools should handle sexual harassment allegations has focused on how the rules expand the rights of those accused — and they do. But the most significant parts of the proposal have nothing to do with protecting students, accused or otherwise. Instead, if allowed to go into effect, the new rules would, above all, protect schools.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 357, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 357}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 609, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1218, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/27/opinion/27BORGER02/27BORGER02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Betsy DeVos’s New Harassment Rules Protect Schools, Not Students', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rules That Protect Schools, Not Students', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Title IX (Gender Discrimination Legislation)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeVos, Elizabeth (1958- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-27T16:03:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Bolger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bolger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fffa3e2-9ec9-5d99-b94b-164171ef85c0', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fffa3e2-9ec9-5d99-b94b-164171ef85c0'}, {'abstract': 'His ready-to-wear collections reflect a lifelong fascination with Americana as seen through “the outsider perspective of an Englishman in New York.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/27/t-magazine/stuart-vevers-coach-inspiration.html', 'snippet': 'His ready-to-wear collections reflect a lifelong fascination with Americana as seen through “the outsider perspective of an Englishman in New York.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Coach’s creative director, Stuart Vevers, first started paying attention to clothes at the Manchester and Leeds nightclubs he visited as a teenager. “Before that, I was a kid from a working-class family in Northern England who didn’t know the fashion industry even existed,” he says. “Then all of a sudden, I was obsessively dressing in paisley shirts and vintage velvet blazers and customizing my jeans.” At 18, he left his home in Cumbria to study fashion at London’s University of Westminster, graduating with an assistant job at Calvin Klein in New York (“I showed up at the interviewer’s hotel unannounced and begged her to look at my portfolio”). He returned to Europe two years later, eventually landing as the head of accessories at Givenchy and then at Louis Vuitton, where Marc Jacobs was his mentor. With the launches of Givenchy’s pumpkin bags and the multicolor monograms Takashi Murakami collaborated on with Louis Vuitton, Vevers became synonymous with the early 2000s It-bag phenomenon and was hired to reinvigorate Mulberry in 2004, followed by Loewe in 2007. Those roles made him an obvious choice for Coach, the American heritage brand that got its start in 1941 producing billfolds and briefcases inspired by baseball gloves. “Although I use our history as a jumping-off point, I felt we needed to radically catapult forward,” says Vevers, 45. “On day one, I decided to think beyond just accessories.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/27/t-magazine/27tmag-vevers-slide-606D/27tmag-vevers-slide-606D-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/11/27/t-magazine/27tmag-vevers-slide-606D/27tmag-vevers-slide-606D-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xl