park rangers in the Santa Monica Mountains, was last seen in one of the first areas to be scorched in the blaze.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the morning of Nov. 9, a wildfire busted out of a small area outside Los Angeles, jumped Highway 101 and ignited the jagged hills of the Santa Monica Mountains. In its fiery path were thousands of homes and residents, and a mountain lion named P-74.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1499, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/04/us/28xp-lion/28xp-lion-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mountain Lion Tracked in Southern California Likely Died in Woolsey Fire, Park Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Last Signal From P-74: The Mountain Lion Who Couldn’t Outrun A California Wildfire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wildfires', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Monica Mountains National Recreation Area (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mountain Lions and Cougars', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-27T20:14:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Haag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/353e7de2-b7ea-5c88-9da3-4f15ed6c8c37', 'word_count': 836, 'uri': 'nyt://article/353e7de2-b7ea-5c88-9da3-4f15ed6c8c37'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Serre’s slender work of fiction, recently translated from the French, is about three carnal but innocent women working for a large family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/27/books/review-governesses-anne-serre.html', 'snippet': 'Anne Serre’s slender work of fiction, recently translated from the French, is about three carnal but innocent women working for a large family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most so-called unusual novels are unusual in a few usual ways. It’s generally a matter of giving us too much or too little: drowning us in detail or withholding it; presenting a narrator who is a cipher or one who can’t stop clowning. Sometimes there’s a quirky framing device or (invariably ill-advised) experimentation with the book’s font or design. Whatever the case, whatever the tactic, these books aren’t as new as they strain to be; they’re variations on the familiar — fresh haircuts on faces we know.', 'print_sect