, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frotman, Seth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-28T15:01:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacy Cowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c9fd914-d4f8-57eb-9375-37c018a39539', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c9fd914-d4f8-57eb-9375-37c018a39539'}, {'abstract': 'Jibade-Khalil Huffman’s video installation; Lorraine O’Grady’s collage poems revisited; and a last-chance look at Nona Faustine’s photographs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/28/arts/design/what-to-see-in-new-york-art-galleries-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Jibade-Khalil Huffman’s video installation; Lorraine O’Grady’s collage poems revisited; and a last-chance look at Nona Faustine’s photographs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jibade-Khalil Huffman started off as a poet and he uses images in his video installations as a sort of grammar, composing phrases, sentences and paragraphs with found or filmed footage. Rather than being watched as a single video or viewed on one or two screens, “Black Twitter in the Age of Chaos,” the 45-minute work that dominates Mr. Huffman’s show, “Tempo,” at the Kitchen, is dispersed over nine screens mounted on the wall or placed on the gallery floor. Sampled, spliced or composed sound comes at you from above rather than from any one source. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/11/30/