812a', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60eaa059-9c89-54da-ba7c-832f4e11812a'}, {'abstract': 'With seven hit novels, now the basis of a blockbuster TV show, Volker Kutscher has helped ignite a debate in Germany about whether history is repeating itself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/30/world/europe/weimar-germany-kutscher-babylon-berlin.html', 'snippet': 'With seven hit novels, now the basis of a blockbuster TV show, Volker Kutscher has helped ignite a debate in Germany about whether history is repeating itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Like many people, Volker Kutscher reads daily newspapers to keep up with things. What’s less usual is that the two papers he relies on, the Vossische Zeitung and the Berliner Tageblatt, have been out of print for more than seven decades.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/merlin_147518064_2332c009-eeb1-47c7-a0fc-d8e04e92c3dd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/merlin_147518064_2332c009-eeb1-47c7-a0fc-d8e04e92c3dd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/merlin_147518064_2332c009-eeb1-47c7-a0fc-d8e04e92c3dd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/merlin_147518064_2332c009-eeb1-47c7-a0fc-d8e04e92c3dd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/01kutscher-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/01kutscher-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/01/world/01kutscher-2-print/01kutscher-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Man Who Brought Weimar Into Germans’ Living Rooms', 'kicker': 'The Saturday Profile', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Writer Who Made Weimar the Talk of Germany', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Twenties', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alternative for Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cologne (Germany)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kutscher, Volker', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-30T16:11:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher F. Schuetze', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Schuetze', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95448e16-0635-5561-8ca4-bb17e7d734fe', 'word_count': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95448e16-0635-5561-8ca4-bb17e7d734fe'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Lutes’s “Berlin,” over two decades in the making, explores a society on the verge of collapse. One of two books reviewed in Ed Park’s Graphic Content column.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/30/books/review/jason-lutes-berlin.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Lutes’s “Berlin,” over two decades in the making, explores a society on the verge of collapse. One of two books reviewed in Ed Park’s Graphic Content column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every 40 pages or so in Jason Lutes’s BERLIN (Drawn + Quarterly, $49.95), a sequence appears that seems to hold the key to this massive graphic novel. In one of the earliest, a drawing instructor stands before a chalkboard, lecturing on the use of perspective. “The observer’s location is what really determines the placement of a vanishing point,” he explains, and asks his students to consider the views out of three adjacent windows in the studio. “Stand still in front of each and imagine it as a framed picture.” The same scene looks different depending on the observer’s position. It’s a metaphor, of course, for the book itself — an opus of more than 500 pages set in the late 1920s in the titular city, teeming with journalists and junkmen, artists and runaways, fiery rabble-rousers and burnt-out cases from the First World War, perceiving the city with their own nervous systems.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '63', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 518}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 885, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1550, 'width': 1794, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/books/review/02Park-01_SUB02/02Park-01_SUB02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Long Visit to Weimar-Era Berlin Through an Epic Graphic Novel', 'kicker': 'Graphic Content', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Graphic Content', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-11-30T16:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ed Park', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Park', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcc9d7c1-2220-5c3f-a32f-b024f5d286e1', 'word_count': 1164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcc9d7c1-2220-5c3f-a32f-b024f5d286e1'}, {'abstract': '